        "tekst": "Read: **Macionis & Plummer, chapter 11**, Racism, ethnicities and migration (pp. 327-362).\n\nThis page works through the whole chapter in the book's own order: the opening story about Bosnia, all main sections, the boxes (Du Bois, Michael Mann on ethnic cleansing, the Bosnian testimony, the UK chronology, the veil debate, the Roma and the skinheads), the tables and figures, the summary and the \"big debate\" on Islamophobia. If you do not have the book at hand, you can do the material here.\n\n**Where this comes back.** The first half of Society & Politics is sociology (Macionis & Plummer), and **the midterm covers that half only**. Chapter 11 is the second \"identity\" session, after chapter 7 (the social construction of everyday life). It builds directly on it: the Thomas theorem from chapter 7 returns here as the engine of the vicious cycle of prejudice and discrimination.\n\nExam questions on this chapter typically pair a **concept with a name** (Merton and the four types, Adorno and the authoritarian personality, Bogardus and social distance, Barker and the new racism, Castles and Miller and the five trends, Hall and the three responses) or ask you to **apply a model** to a case (which pattern of interaction, which migration model, which theory of prejudice)."
