        "titel": "1. The social significance of race and ethnicities",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "**The opening story: Bosnia.** The chapter opens with Bosnia, a federal state of the former Yugoslavia and a country of deep multicultural and ethnic mixture. Its historian Noel Malcolm (1996) wrote that there is no such thing as a typical Bosnian face: many peoples contributed to that human mosaic. In a thousand years it was touched by Rome, Charlemagne, the Ottomans and the Austro-Hungarians, and it harboured Western and Eastern Christianity, Judaism and Islam. In 1991 the three main groups were **Muslims (44 per cent), Serbs (31 per cent) and Croats (17 per cent)**.\n\nIn **1992** a civil war broke out between the Serbs (Orthodox Christians), the Croats (Catholics) and the Bosnian Muslims. It cost an estimated **500,000 lives**, produced some **3.5 million refugees**, and involved the systematic rape of thousands of Muslim women and the destruction of cities, mosques and churches. Each group claimed territory and tried to \"cleanse\" the others; the largest ethnic cleansing was of Bosnian Muslims by Serbs. Only in **1995**, after much international intervention, was a settlement reached: the **Dayton Agreement** (Map 11.2 shows the Dayton line dividing the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina from the Republika Srpska).\n\nThe book draws a wider lesson: most wars today are not between states but **within** them. Between 1989 and 1992 there were 80 armed conflicts, only three of them between countries. The book then lists ethnic conflicts across the world (Ukrainians, Moldavians and Azerbaijanis after the Soviet collapse, Arabs and Jews, South Africa, Kurds in Turkey, Sudan, Tamils and Sinhalese in Sri Lanka, Hutus and Tutsis in Rwanda, Sunni and Shi'a in Iraq, Israelis and Palestinians), plus urban unrest such as the **Los Angeles riots of 1992** and the **French suburbs in autumn 2005**, when some 6,000 cars were burned in 12 nights in a conflict between marginalised youth and the French state. France, with 7-10 per cent ethnic minorities (70 per cent of them Muslim), followed an assimilationist and integrationist policy that was seen as not working.\n\nThe chapter's aim: to examine racism and ethnicity as **social constructs**, show how they shaped history, and explain why they still play such a central role.\n\n**What's in a name? Problems of terminology.** People use \"race\" and \"ethnicity\" imprecisely and interchangeably, and there is no agreed \"correct\" language. Some prefer the words groups use themselves (\"black\" in the UK, \"people of colour\" in the US), but those groups disagree internally. **Kum-Kum Bhavnani** (1993) proposes \"black\" as a **political category** for all non-white groups who experience racism, including Pakistani, Bangladeshi and Indian groups, as a unifying force for a black movement. **Tariq Modood** (1994) disagrees: \"black\" creates a false sense of unity, is used inconsistently (sometimes only for people of African descent), most Asians do not use it, and what is needed is a language of **Asian pride** with its own roots.\n\n**Race and racialisation.** \"Race\" is a muddled and even dangerous concept, and a **modern** one: it came into use between the late eighteenth and mid-nineteenth centuries. It usually refers to a category of people who share **biologically transmitted traits that members of a society deem socially significant**: skin colour, facial features, hair texture, body shape. Those differences are superficial; all humans are one biological species. The variety of traits is the product of migration and intermarriage. The most striking variation appears in the **Middle East** (western Asia), a historical crossroads of migration; striking uniformity appears among isolated peoples such as the island-dwelling **Japanese**. No society lacks genetic mixture, and contact will speed up blending.\n\nIn the nineteenth century **A. de Gobineau** (1816-1882), in his *Essay on the Inequality of the Human Races* (1853), developed a three-part scheme: \"Caucasian\" (light skin, fine hair), \"Negroid\" (darker skin, coarser curlier hair) and \"Mongoloid\" (yellow or brown skin, eyelid folds). This \"race science\" claimed Caucasians were more intelligent and moral. Sociologists reject such schemes as both **misleading** (no population is biologically pure) and **politically dangerous** (they fuel racism). Most sociologists therefore reject the idea of race itself.\n\nYet people everywhere classify each other racially. Sociologists call this process of ranking people on the basis of presumed race **racialisation**; it is at the heart of a system of inequality and exclusion. Because so much is at stake, societies make racial labels much clearer than the facts allow. Earlier in the twentieth century many southern US states legally defined anyone with as little as **one thirty-second** African ancestry (one great-great-great-grandparent) as \"coloured\". Today US law lets parents declare a child's race, if they wish to at all.\n\n**Ethnicity and the diaspora.** **Ethnicity** is a shared **cultural and historical heritage**: common ancestors, language or religion that together give a distinctive social identity. It is a useful term, but not when it is only applied to \"others\": **everybody has an ethnicity**. The book illustrates with language: over a million foreign students in French schools and three-quarters of a million in Germany; around **100 minority languages in Britain**, about a quarter taught in English schools; over 30 million people in the US speaking a language other than English at home (Crystal, 2005). Religion also marks ethnicity: Catholics of Spanish, Italian and Polish ancestry, Eastern Orthodox of Greek, Ukrainian and Russian ancestry, more than 6 million Jewish Americans, several million Europeans with a Muslim heritage.\n\nRace and ethnicity may overlap (Gujarati Hindus can have distinctive physical traits and cultural attributes), but they differ: race rests on a (muddled) sense of **biological** difference, ethnicity on **cultural** heritage. Ethnic distinctiveness should not be viewed as racial: Jews are sometimes called a race although they are distinctive in religion and in a history of persecution (Goldsby, 1977).\n\nEthnicity is also **more variable and mixed** than race. Most people identify with more than one background, which produces **hyphenated identities** such as \"Asian-British\" or \"Black-African\". \"Asians in Britain\" are in fact many communities (Table 11.1). And people can **change** their ethnicity over time: many West Indian immigrants to England became less \"West Indian\"; others highlighted their background through Rastafarianism; many people with Irish ancestry renewed interest in it. Ethnicity is fluid, which leads to studying migration, hybridity and **diasporas**: the dispersal of a population from its homeland into other areas.\n\n**Minorities.** A racial or ethnic **minority** is a category of people, distinguished by physical or cultural traits, who are **socially disadvantaged**. The term has widened to people with disabilities, women (some argue) and gays and lesbians. Minorities have **two major characteristics**:\n\n1. **A shared distinctive identity.** Race is highly visible and virtually impossible to change, so racial minorities are keenly aware of it. Ethnicity can be changed and its significance varies (compare many Reform Jews, who downplay it, with many Orthodox Jews living in enclaves).\n2. **Subordination.** Minorities typically have less income, lower occupational prestige and less schooling. Class, race, ethnicity and gender overlap and reinforce each other.\n\nNot every member of a minority is disadvantaged, but race or ethnicity often works as a **master status** that overshadows personal accomplishment."
