        "titel": "2. Prejudice and racism",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "Why do people hold negative views of others based on race or ethnicity? The book uses three ideas: prejudice, stereotyping and racism.\n\n**Prejudice** is a **rigid and irrational generalisation about an entire category of people**. It is an **attitude**, a prejudgement applied indiscriminately and inflexibly with little regard for the facts. Prejudices exist about class, sex, sexual orientation, age, political affiliation, race and ethnicity. They can be **positive** (exaggerating the virtues of people like us) or **negative** (condemning those who differ). Negative prejudice runs along a continuum from mild aversion to outright hostility. Because attitudes are rooted in culture, **everyone has some prejudice**, and minorities too hold prejudices, about whites and often about other minorities.\n\n**Stereotypes.** Prejudices combine into a **stereotype** (from the Greek *stereo*, \"hard\" or \"solid\"): a prejudicial, exaggerated description applied to **every** person in a category. Because stereotypes involve emotions (love and loyalty towards in-groups, hate and fear towards out-groups), they resist contradictory evidence. The book's example is the stereotype of the poor as lazy \"spongers\": in reality most poor people are children, women, working adults or elderly people (Chapter 10). In the US, half of white people stereotyped African-Americans as lacking motivation (NORC, 1994). That assumes disadvantage is a personal deficiency, and ignores that most poor Americans are white and most African-Americans work as hard as anyone and are not poor. The grain of truth (black people are more likely to be poor) is built into a rigid attitude that **grossly distorts reality**.\n\n**Racism** is a powerful and destructive form of prejudice: **the belief that one racial category is innately superior or inferior to another**. It has pervaded world history: the ancient Greeks, the peoples of India and the Chinese all readily saw \"others\" as inferior. It is probably found in every society today; examples in the book include Aboriginals, Pacific Islanders and Chinese in Australia, and Somalis and other refugees in Denmark.\n\n**Racism and social domination.** Claims of innate inferiority have served to **justify subordination**. By the end of the nineteenth century European nations and the US had built vast empires, brutally subjugating peoples with the claim that they were less human. In the twentieth century racism was central to the Nazi idea of an \"Aryan super-race\" of blond, blue-eyed Germans destined to rule, which encouraged the systematic slaughter of some **6 million European Jews** and millions of Poles, Roma, homosexuals and people with physical and mental disabilities. More recently, racial conflict has intensified in Western Europe with immigration from former colonies and Eastern Europe, and growing intolerance has fuelled a resurgence of Nazi-style rhetoric in Germany, France, Britain and elsewhere."
