        "type": "voorbeeld",
        "tekst": "**Box: Classical social thinkers, W.E.B. Du Bois on race and conflict**\n\n**William Edward Burghardt Du Bois** (1868-1963) is a sociological pioneer who has not received the attention he deserves. Born to a poor Massachusetts family, he was the only person of African descent from his small town to go to college. After Fisk University in Nashville he went to Harvard, and earned the **first Harvard doctorate awarded to a person of colour**.\n\nHe believed sociology should address contemporary problems, and race was for him the paramount one. African-Americans, he argued, live with a **\"double consciousness\"**: unlike whites, who can be simply \"Americans\", they can never escape identification by colour. Opposed to racial separation, he was a **founding member of the NAACP** (National Association for the Advancement of Colored People).\n\nAt the University of Pennsylvania he wrote the classic **The Philadelphia Negro: A Social Study (1899)**, a systematic study of the city's African-American community, its strengths and its problems. Against the current of the time (especially Spencer's social Darwinism) he **rejected the notion of black inferiority** and attributed the problems to **white prejudice**. He also criticised successful black people for segregating themselves from the mass in their eagerness for white acceptance. Optimistic at first, he grew bitter that little had changed, and at 93 left the US for **Ghana**, where he died two years later."
