        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "4. Discrimination",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "**Discrimination** is **any action that involves treating various categories of people unequally**. Prejudice is attitude; discrimination is **behaviour**. It can be **positive** (special advantages) or **negative** (obstacles), and ranges from subtle to blatant.\n\n**Merton's typology.** Prejudice and discrimination often, but not always, go together. **Robert Merton** (1976) described four types, using the example of personnel managers:\n\n- the **active bigot**: prejudiced and discriminates (refuses to employ members of a minority);\n- the **timid bigot**: prejudiced but does not discriminate, for example out of fear of legal action;\n- the **fair-weather liberal**: not prejudiced, but discriminates when it is expedient, for example when a superior demands it;\n- the **all-weather liberal**: free of both prejudice and discrimination.\n\n**Not all discrimination is wrong.** In the basic sense of making distinctions, we discriminate all the time (preferring personalities, looks, talents). Societies praise some discrimination and condemn other forms. Universities rightly favour more able applicants, consistent with the cultural expectation that the greatest rewards go to ability and effort. But favouring Christians over Jews regardless of talent would be morally wrong and illegal, unless religion is directly relevant (a religious mission). Principles of fairness change with economic development: in low-income countries people routinely favour family, clan, religious group and village (a moral duty to \"look after their own\"); in high-income societies norms elevate the individual, so **achievement rather than ascription** guides fairness, and organisations forbid nepotism and conflicts of interest.\n\n**Institutional discrimination.** Far greater harm comes not from individuals but from **institutional discrimination**: **bias in attitudes or action inherent in the operation of society's institutions**, including schools, hospitals, the police and the workplace. Until the US Supreme Court's **Brown decision of 1954**, the principle of **\"separate but equal\"** legally justified separate schools for black and white children: an educational caste system. Even after Brown, most US students still attend schools where one race predominates; a **1991** court decision recognised that neighbourhood schools would never give equal education while neighbourhoods remain racially segregated (African-Americans mostly in city centres, whites and Asian Americans in separate suburbs).\n\n**The Macpherson Report.** In the UK, **Stephen Lawrence**, a young black man, was murdered by a gang of white youths at a bus stop on **22 April 1993**. His family complained the murder was not properly investigated and that the police behaved in a racist way. The inquiry by **Sir William Macpherson (1999)** concluded that although individual officers may not have been deliberately prejudiced, the **Metropolitan Police Service was \"institutionally racist\"**. Macpherson defined institutional racism as the **collective failure of an organisation** to provide an appropriate and professional service to people because of their colour, culture or ethnic origin. It can be seen in **processes, attitudes and behaviour** that amount to discrimination through **unwitting prejudice, ignorance, thoughtlessness and racist stereotyping** which disadvantage minority ethnic people. The report became very influential in debates on policing and racism in the UK.\n\nNote the key words: *collective*, *unwitting*. Institutional racism does not require any individual racist intent; it lives in how the organisation works.\n\n**Prejudice and discrimination: the vicious cycle.** The two reinforce each other. **W.I. Thomas** explained why with the **Thomas theorem** (from chapter 7): **if situations are defined as real, they are real in their consequences**. Stereotypes become real to those who believe them, and sometimes even to their victims. **Power** matters: some categories can enforce their prejudices. White prejudice does not produce *innate* inferiority, but it can produce *social* inferiority (poverty, low-prestige jobs, poor segregated housing). If that disadvantage is then read as proof that minorities \"do not measure up\", a new round of prejudice and discrimination follows, even across generations (Figure 11.1)."
