        "tekst": "**Why this matters in safety and security (own explanation, not from the book)**\n\nInstitutional discrimination is one of the most practical concepts in this chapter for your future field, because the organisations in it (police, border control, security companies, inspectorates, tax and fraud units) work with risk profiles and selection rules. A rule that looks neutral can still systematically disadvantage a group: that is exactly Macpherson's \"unwitting prejudice\" and \"processes\".\n\nDutch examples you can connect this to are the debate on **ethnic profiling** in police stops and the **childcare benefits scandal** (toeslagenaffaire), in which risk selection that used nationality as an indicator hit families with a migration background; the Dutch government itself later acknowledged institutional racism at the tax authority. The vicious cycle also explains how profiling can feed itself: if you check one group more, you find more offences in that group, which \"confirms\" the profile."
