        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "5. Majority and minority: patterns of interaction",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "Social scientists describe interaction between racial and ethnic categories with **four models**: pluralism, assimilation, segregation and genocide.\n\n**Pluralism** is a state in which racial and ethnic minorities are **distinct but have social parity**: socially different, but sharing basic resources more or less equally. Most Western societies are pluralistic to some degree: schooling and services for all, equal standing before the law, and \"ethnic villages\" in big cities (in London Arab, Chinese, Maltese, Greek, Italian, Jewish, West Indian and Asian communities; in New York Spanish Harlem, Little Italy and Chinatown; Little Havana in Miami). Pluralism, living with difference, is one goal of **multiculturalism** (chapter 5). But the West is far from fully pluralistic. Only a small proportion of US minorities want to live only with their \"own kind\"; identity is often **forced** on people by others who shun them (Appalachian \"hillbillies\"); and rising immigration has provoked movements such as the campaign to make **English the official US language**, hardly a pluralist strategy. **Switzerland** is a sharper example: German, French and Italian traditions, all three languages officially recognised, and the three categories have roughly the same economic standing (Simpson & Yinger, 1972), though the book notes, critically, that there is little difference in colour.\n\n**Assimilation** is the process by which **minorities gradually adopt the patterns of the dominant culture**: dress, values, religion, language, friends. This is the **\"melting pot\"** model, captured in Israel Zangwill's play (1909), which pictured America as God's crucible melting Europe's nationalities into a new American. The image is **historically misleading**: rather than everyone melting into something new, minorities typically adopt the traits (dress, accent, sometimes names) of the dominant culture of the earliest settlers. **Elites** favour assimilation because it holds them up as the standard. Many immigrants also pursue it, hoping to escape prejudice and move up (Newman, 1973). **Multiculturalists** criticise it for defining minorities as \"the problem\" and making them do all the changing. Some assimilation has happened (Afro-Caribbean and Asian cultures in the UK have taken on key features of British society), but new ethnic villages keep appearing: almost **30 per cent of New Yorkers** are foreign born, the highest in 50 years, so race and ethnicity endure as building blocks of US society (Glazer & Moynihan, 1970; Alba, 1985). Assimilation changes **ethnicity but not race** (many Japanese Americans dropped traditional ways but keep their racial identity). Racial traits diminish over generations through **miscegenation**, reproduction by partners of different racial categories; officially recorded interracial births in the US were **4 per cent** and rising.\n\n**Segregation** is the **physical and social separation of categories of people**. Some minorities segregate **voluntarily** (the Amish of Pennsylvania), but mostly majorities segregate minorities **involuntarily** by excluding them, in housing, schools, jobs, hospitals and even cemeteries. Pluralism means distinctiveness **without** disadvantage; segregation enforces separation **to the detriment** of a minority. **South African apartheid** is the rigid, pervasive example, created and brutally enforced by the white minority. In the US, slavery gave way to separate lodging, schools and transport (\"Jim Crow\" laws, dismantled only in the early 1960s). Brown (1954) reduced **de jure** segregation (\"by law\"), but **de facto** segregation (\"in fact\") continues. **Karl and Alma Taeuber** (1965) measured residential segregation in more than 200 US cities on a scale from 0 (mixing in all neighbourhoods) to 100 (in none) and found an average of **86.2**; later research found only a slight decrease, and even high-income African-Americans find colour closes off housing (Wilson, 1991). **Douglas Massey and Nancy Denton** (1989) showed that separation covers a host of life experiences: many inner-city African-Americans have little social contact of any kind with the outside world. This **hypersegregation** affects about one fifth of African-Americans but only a small fraction of comparably poor whites. Segregation means second-class citizenship, and individuals have fought it: on **1 December 1955 Rosa Parks** refused to give up her seat on a Montgomery, Alabama bus, was arrested and fined **$14**, and the bus boycott that followed ended legal segregation on the buses (King, 1969).\n\n**Genocide** is **the systematic annihilation of one category of people by another**. A more recent term is **ethnic cleansing**, used especially for Bosnia. Despite violating nearly every moral standard, it recurs throughout history. From the sixteenth century the Spanish, Portuguese, English, French and Dutch colonisation of the Americas decimated native populations, some through killing sprees, most through European diseases they had no defence against. **R.J. Rummel**, in *Death by Government* (1996), coined **democide** for mass murder by governments. He argues democracies commit less democide than other regimes, and estimates some **169 million** victims of state murder in the twentieth century: the Soviet state about **61 million**, communist China **35 million**, the Nazi state **21 million**, the Chinese Nationalist regime some **10 million**, Japan's military **5 million**, Pol Pot's Cambodia **2 million** (a quarter of the population, 1975-1980), nearly **2 million in Turkey**, and many more elsewhere."
