        "type": "voorbeeld",
        "tekst": "**Box: Research in action, Michael Mann on ethnic cleansing**\n\nIn *The Dark Side of Democracy: Explaining Ethnic Cleansing* (2005), **Michael Mann** traces the history of genocide and state murder (Nazis, Stalin, Mao) and tries to build a broader theory. Murderous cleansing appears throughout history (Assyrians, Greeks, Romans, Goths, Mongols, Genghis Khan, Attila, Ivan the Terrible), but Mann's surprising claim is that it is **distinctly modern**: it has become a **feature of the democratic process** itself, when \"the people\" is defined ethnically. Twentieth-century ethnic conflicts produced some **70 million refugees**, and in 2003 such conflicts were found from Bosnia, Kosovo and Chechnya to Sri Lanka, Sudan, Rwanda, Congo and the Basque Country. The Nazis are estimated to have killed some 20 million people in the last four of their twelve years.\n\nMann identifies **four linked networks of power** that organise this violence: **ideological** (values and rituals), **economic** (material interests), **military** (lethal violence) and **political** (central, territorial regulation of life). Societies in the middle of **democratisation** are in the greatest turmoil, because old ethnic \"enemy minorities\" are easily found; these are largely in the global South. The North has become more stable, after its own earlier period of conflict (Catholic against Protestant, for example)."
