        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "6. Migration, ethnicity and race",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "We live in a world of **100 million immigrants, 19 million of them refugees** (Sowell, 1996). Migration patterns reveal how societies work: they explain demography (chapter 24) and the dynamics between groups, because migrant groups are often singled out for racialisation and discrimination. It is hard to find a society without outsider ethnic groups.\n\nMigration has always existed: voluntary, planned or forced. As Stola (in Sowell, 1996) put it, deportation, evacuation, exile, forcible repatriation, compulsory transfer and panic-stricken flight are an essential part of European history. Distances used to be short; modern travel covers vast ones. Sometimes a people is forcibly dispersed (the Jews): these are the **diasporas**. Sometimes peoples are assimilated; sometimes permanent settlers are refused.\n\n**Four patterns of modern migration:**\n\n- **The classical model** (United States, Canada, Australia): countries encourage migration and see immigrants as **future citizens**.\n- **The colonial model** (France, the Netherlands, the UK): migration mainly from former **colonies**, whose migrants have a privileged position with potential citizenship rights.\n- **The guest worker model** (Germany, Belgium): migration is **temporary**, for work, **without** potential citizen rights, with weaker employment conditions and job security.\n- **The \"illegal\" model**: found in most countries; people live outside official society and are pushed into the lowest paid, most temporary and demanding work.\n\n**Four phases of migration** (Castles & Miller, 1993), which fit the classical pattern best:\n\n1. **Temporary labour migration** of young workers who send money home and feel they still belong to the homeland.\n2. **Prolonged stay** and new social networks.\n3. **Family reunion**, long-term settlement issues and new ethnic communities.\n4. **Permanent settlement** (depending on government policy).\n\nThis is an **ideal type**, much less relevant to refugees or highly skilled migrants.\n\n**European patterns of migration.** Europe's history is the history of its migratory peoples, and people from Africa, India and the Caribbean have lived in Europe for hundreds of years. The book identifies **three waves**:\n\n- **Wave 1, the European Exodus**: around the turn of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries some **52 million Europeans left**, 72 per cent settling in North America (Soysal, 1994).\n- **Wave 2, the European Inflow** after the Second World War, with two kinds of recruitment. **Colonial workers**: in the UK from Ireland, the West Indies, South Asia and East Africa; in France by 1970 some 90,000 Tunisians, 140,000 Moroccans and over 600,000 Algerians; in the Netherlands some **300,000 Indonesians** resettled from the former Dutch East Indies (Castles & Miller, 1993). And **guest workers**, \"guests\" expected to leave when the work ended, without political or economic rights. They formed large parts of the manual workforce and were central to Europe's reconstruction. They can be seen as an **industrial reserve army**: a disadvantaged section of labour supplied cheaply when extra demand arises.\n- **Wave 3, after 1973**: a marked decline, with new restrictive legislation (as in the UK). About **15 million** migrants in Europe are \"foreigners\" without formal citizenship (Soysal, 1994). Foreign workers made up **2 per cent** of the labour force in Denmark in 1990 but **18 per cent** in Switzerland. **Sweden**, with little classic migration, adopted a policy in **1975** stressing equality between immigrants and Swedes, freedom of cultural choice, and cooperation between majority and minorities. Figure 11.2 shows the balance of home nationals and minority populations in 13 European countries.\n\n**Refugees and migration in crisis.** War, genocide, conflict and disaster force people from their homes. The book describes three crises (as of about 2004):\n\n- **Afghanistan** (about 28 million people, several million in camps in Pakistan and Iran): a stable monarchy from the 1930s to the 1970s, overthrown by Marxist rebels and the USSR, then an Islamic Republic in 1992, then the Taliban in 1996; after 9/11 a US and British bombing campaign; by late 2004 democratic elections seemed possible.\n- **Sudan** (38 million, then Africa's largest country): an Arab Muslim majority in the north and Christian or animist groups (Dinka, Nuba, Zande, Nuer) in the oil-rich south; British colonial rule 1898-1956, then constant war between south and north, millions homeless. In 2004 it was seen as one of the worst humanitarian crises. (The book's death toll is a serious underestimate; see Before you start.)\n- **Rwanda** (7 million, life expectancy about 38.5): originally Twa, then Hutu, with Tutsi rule from the fifteenth century and alternating rule since; the book describes the massacre of over 500,000 Tutsi and some 2 million, mainly Hutu, refugees fleeing to neighbouring countries.\n\n**Box: Speaking lives, living through ethnic cleansing.** Psychiatrist **Stevan Weine** (1999) spent years recording testimonies of Bosnians who survived ethnic cleansing. The testimony in the book, from a survivor called H., describes a prison camp: starvation, beatings, overcrowding in which prisoners died, mass executions of which he was one of three survivors, looting of property through forced \"wills\", and the murder of the prisoners forced to dispose of bodies so there would be no witnesses. It also shows the moral grey zones: a fellow prisoner forced to beat others to save himself, whom H. refuses to condemn, and a violent prisoner killed collectively by a thousand inmates. The sociological point of the box is that genocide is not an abstraction: it is organised, it involves ordinary people on all sides, and it leaves lasting trauma in survivors.\n\n**Future trends of migration.** **Castles and Miller** (2003) predict **five key features**:\n\n1. **Globalisation of migration**: more and more countries affected.\n2. **Acceleration**: growing numbers in all major regions.\n3. **Differentiation**: refugees, labour migrants and permanent settlers all at once.\n4. **Feminisation**: women play a much larger role (once mainly men migrated first).\n5. **Politicisation**: migration raises issues of religion, domestic politics, and national and international **security**.\n\nMore ethnic diversity is likely, and with it, possibly, **more racism**.\n\n**North America: the land of migration, and the coming majority?** Europe's figures are small next to the US, \"the modern land of migration\" (Table 11.2; Ellis Island was the entry point for most migrants). The argument is made that \"white\" groups are about to become a **minority**. In the 1980s **Manhattan** gained a **minority-majority**: people of African, Asian, Latino and other descent together became the majority. The same happened in **186 counties** (some 6 per cent). By **2050**, according to some projections, minorities will be the majority. The 2000 Census (Figure 11.3) shows the white non-Hispanic share falling by over 5 percentage points between 1990 and 2000 (from 75.6 to 69.1 per cent), while Asians, blacks and Latinos grew; over half the increase was in **California, Florida and Texas**. **Stephan Thernstrom** (1998) disputes the projection: it assumes immigration stays high despite forecasts of a downturn, and that immigrants' high birth rates continue, whereas over time immigrants tend to behave like everyone else."
