      {
        "type": "oefening",
        "id": "soc-c3-oef-1",
        "niveau": "basis",
        "vraag": "Place each situation in Merton's typology and explain your choice.\n\na) A landlord thinks tenants from a certain background are \"unreliable\", but rents to them anyway because he fears a discrimination complaint.\nb) A recruiter has no negative views of any group, but drops applicants with a foreign-sounding name because her manager told her \"the client prefers Dutch names\".\nc) A shop owner refuses to hire young men of a particular ethnicity because he believes they steal.\nd) An HR officer neither holds negative views nor treats applicants differently.",
        "antwoord": "**a) Timid bigot.** Prejudiced (he believes the stereotype) but does not discriminate, and only because of fear of legal consequences, not conviction. Note the book's point: prejudice and discrimination do not always go together.\n\n**b) Fair-weather liberal.** Not prejudiced, but discriminates when it is expedient, here because a superior demands it. This is also a good example of how discrimination can spread through an organisation without individual hatred, which points towards **institutional** discrimination.\n\n**c) Active bigot.** Prejudiced and discriminates: the stereotype is turned directly into exclusion.\n\n**d) All-weather liberal.** Free of both prejudice and discrimination."
      },
      {
        "type": "oefening",
        "id": "soc-c3-oef-2",
        "niveau": "basis",
        "vraag": "A classmate says: \"Race and ethnicity are the same thing, they both describe where your family comes from.\" Correct this statement using the chapter, and give an example that shows the difference.",
        "antwoord": "The statement confuses two different concepts.\n\n**Race** refers to a category of people who share **biologically transmitted traits** that a society considers significant, such as skin colour or hair texture. Sociologists see it as a social construction: the traits are superficial, all humans are one species, and nineteenth-century schemes such as Gobineau's are discredited. Race is highly visible and virtually impossible to change.\n\n**Ethnicity** is a shared **cultural and historical heritage**: ancestry, language, religion. Everybody has one, it is often multiple and hyphenated, and people can change it over time.\n\n**Example from the book.** Many Japanese Americans have **assimilated** and discarded their traditional way of life (their ethnicity changed) while keeping their racial identity (their race did not). Another example: Jews are sometimes wrongly called a \"race\", although their distinctiveness lies in religion and a shared history of persecution, which makes it ethnic, not racial.\n\nSo the two can overlap (Gujarati Hindus may share both physical traits and cultural attributes), but they are not the same."
      },
      {
        "type": "oefening",
        "id": "soc-c3-oef-3",
        "niveau": "gevorderd",
        "vraag": "A police unit uses a risk model to decide which cars to stop. The model gives extra weight to \"young men in older cars in neighbourhood X\". Neighbourhood X has a large Moroccan-Dutch population. No officer in the unit holds racist views, and the model does not mention ethnicity.\n\nCan this be institutional discrimination? Use Macpherson's definition and the vicious cycle in your answer.",
        "antwoord": "**Yes, it can**, and the chapter gives the tools to show why.\n\n**Macpherson's definition** does not require individual prejudice. Institutional racism is the **collective failure** of an organisation to provide an appropriate and professional service because of colour, culture or ethnic origin, visible in **processes** and behaviour that discriminate through **unwitting** prejudice, ignorance, thoughtlessness or stereotyping. Here the process (the risk model) combines characteristics that act as a **proxy** for ethnicity: neighbourhood X plus young men means Moroccan-Dutch young men are stopped far more often, even though nobody intends it and ethnicity is never named. That fits \"unwitting\" and \"processes\" exactly. The fact that officers are not racist is irrelevant to the definition, just as Macpherson accepted that individual Met officers may not have been deliberately prejudiced.\n\n**The vicious cycle (Figure 11.1)** shows how the model can feed itself. Stage 1: the group is checked more (discrimination). Stage 2: because you only find what you look for, more offences are recorded in that group, and more young men from it get a record, which harms their chances of work (social disadvantage). Stage 3: the higher numbers are read as proof that \"this group is more criminal\", which confirms the risk model and justifies even more checks. The **Thomas theorem** is at work: the situation defined as real (this group is risky) becomes real in its consequences.\n\n**What a professional recommendation would include:** test the model for disparate impact per group, compare hit rates (are stops of this group actually more often justified?), remove proxies that do not add predictive value, and involve the community. This is also where the Dutch debate on ethnic profiling and the childcare benefits scandal are relevant: in both, a \"neutral\" selection rule disadvantaged people with a migration background."
      },
      {
        "type": "oefening",
        "id": "soc-c3-oef-4",
        "niveau": "gevorderd",
        "vraag": "After a series of burglaries in a town, a local politician says: \"It is not about race. It is simply that people from culture Y do not share our norms and values. Their culture is incompatible with ours.\"\n\nAnalyse this statement with at least three concepts or authors from the chapter.",
        "antwoord": "**1. Barker's new racism (1981).** This is the textbook example. The politician explicitly distances himself from biological race (\"it is not about race\"), but argues that **cultural difference** is what really matters and that the other culture is **incompatible** with the dominant one. That is exactly how Barker describes the new racism: the same exclusion, justified by culture instead of biology.\n\n**2. Gilroy (1987).** Gilroy warns of a racism that avoids being recognised as racism by lining up \"race\" with **nation** and presenting the nation as a unified cultural community threatened by enemies within. \"Our norms and values\" versus \"their culture\" draws precisely such a national boundary.\n\n**3. Stereotyping.** A few burglaries (possibly by a few people) are turned into a statement about **every** member of culture Y. That is a stereotype: an exaggerated description applied to a whole category, resistant to contrary evidence.\n\n**4. Scapegoat theory (Dollard et al., 1939).** Frustration about crime is directed at a relatively powerless and visible group, a \"safe target\", rather than at more complex causes such as policing capacity, local poverty or organised crime.\n\n**5. Hall's first response.** The statement fits a **defensive strengthening of the dominant identity**, reasserting \"our\" absolute values under perceived threat. Hall predicts this can provoke the second response: a defensive counter-identity among the minority, which deepens the divide.\n\n**A strong conclusion** notes that whether a claim about culture is racist depends on whether it essentialises a whole group and uses that to justify unequal treatment; that is what distinguishes this statement from a sociological analysis of cultural difference."
      },
      {
        "type": "oefening",
        "id": "soc-c3-oef-5",
        "niveau": "gevorderd",
        "vraag": "Apply the migration part of the chapter to the Netherlands. Which of the four models of migration fit Dutch migration history, and which phase of Castles and Miller's model explains why \"guest workers\" often did not leave? What problem does this create for the idea of the guest worker model?",
        "antwoord": "**The models.** The book itself places the Netherlands under the **colonial model**: some **300,000 Indonesians** resettled from the former Dutch East Indies after the Second World War, and migrants from former colonies (the book mentions this pattern; Surinamese and Antilleans are the other Dutch examples) had a privileged position with potential citizenship. But the Netherlands also recruited **guest workers** (from Turkey and Morocco, for example), which fits the **guest worker model**: temporary labour, expected to return, with weaker rights. And like most countries it has **illegal** migration. So the Netherlands is a mix of the colonial and guest worker models, which shows the models are ideal types, not boxes that each country fits neatly.\n\n**The phases.** Castles and Miller's four phases explain what happened. Phase 1: young men came temporarily and sent money home. Phase 2: stays became longer and new networks formed. Phase 3: **family reunion**, which brought wives and children and started long-term settlement and new ethnic communities. Phase 4: permanent settlement. Once family reunion happens, return becomes unlikely: children go to school here and their lives are here.\n\n**The problem for the guest worker model.** The model assumes migration stays **temporary**, and gives migrants no citizen rights because they are \"guests\". The phase model shows that labour migration tends to become permanent. The result is a group that is permanently settled but was never treated as future citizens, which fits the book's description of guest workers as an **industrial reserve army**: cheap labour when needed, without the rights and integration support of the classical model. That gap between legal status and actual permanence is a recurring source of disadvantage and tension."
      },
      {
        "type": "oefening",
        "id": "soc-c3-oef-6",
        "niveau": "gevorderd",
        "vraag": "Compare Adorno's authoritarian personality theory with the cultural theory of prejudice (Bogardus). What does each imply for how a government should fight prejudice, and why does the book lean towards the cultural view?",
        "antwoord": "**Adorno et al. (1950)** see extreme prejudice as a **personality trait** of particular individuals. People prejudiced against one minority were intolerant of all; they were rigidly conventional, saw morality in black and white, were ethnocentric and saw society as naturally hierarchical. The cause lies in upbringing (harsh, cold parents) and little education. **Implication for policy:** target the minority of prejudiced individuals (education, early intervention in upbringing) and treat prejudice as a deviation from a tolerant norm.\n\n**The cultural theory** holds that some prejudice is common to **everyone** because it is embedded in culture. **Bogardus** (1968), measuring **social distance** for over 40 years, found that people across the US ranked groups in much the same order (English, Canadian and Scottish closest, African and Asian descent furthest). Because the pattern was shared, he concluded it was **culturally normative**. **Implication for policy:** you cannot fight prejudice only by targeting a few individuals; you need to change culture itself, for example through multicultural education that moves beyond Eurocentric attitudes (Asante), media representation, and contact between groups.\n\n**Why the book leans to the cultural view:** it asks directly whether, if prejudice is so widespread, we can dismiss intolerance as a trait of a handful of abnormal people, as Adorno did. Its answer is that a more encompassing approach recognises **some bigotry is within us all**, because we adjust to a \"culture of prejudice\". The chapter's conclusion repeats this: prejudice is not the quirk of a few, but a process at work across the world.\n\n**A nuance for a strong answer:** the two are not mutually exclusive. Culture can explain the general level of prejudice, while personality can explain why some people hold it in extreme forms and act on it. And neither theory addresses **power**, which the oppression-of-minorities view adds: whose prejudices get enforced depends on who controls institutions."
      }
